# CASP15 T1158 — The Prostaglandin Pump (8SX8)
---

This is MRP4 — a membrane transporter that pumps
prostaglandins out of cells.

Published in Nature Structural and Molecular Biology
in 2024 by Pourmal and colleagues.

> close
> load 8sx8
---

MRP4 is an ABC transporter — one of those amazing
molecular pumps that use ATP energy to move
molecules across cell membranes.

This one is special because it exports prostaglandins,
the signaling molecules that control pain,
inflammation, and fever.

> spin 0.15
---

Here's the binding pocket with prostaglandin E1 —
PGE1 — caught in the act of being transported.

This was a CASP15 prediction target with four
different ligands, making it one of the most
challenging docking problems in the competition.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Understanding how MRP4 grabs and exports
prostaglandins is crucial for drug design.

Many painkillers and anti-inflammatory drugs
work by blocking prostaglandin synthesis —
but this transporter controls how much
prostaglandin actually reaches its target.

> reset
> spin 0.1
